  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWidespread rain had overspread the middle Ohio Valley this morning, \nwith less consistent coverage in and near the mountains. \nHowever, embedded locally heavier downpours were noted \nregardless of overall coverage.\n\nThis was all on account of flat waves moving up through south to \nsouthwest mid-level flow between long-wave ridging along the east \ncoast, and broad long-wave troughing over the western and central \nU.S. This was pulling a weak surface wave of low pressure over the \nmid-Mississippi Valley, and its associated front extending eastward \nfrom it through the Carolinas, northward toward the middle Ohio \nValley.\n\nThe associated low/mid-level moisture feed was bringing low to mid \n60s dew points and 1.75 inch or greater precipitable water \nvalues northward out of the Gulf and even western Atlantic up \ninto the middle Ohio Valley. Area observed and model soundings \nevinced a moist adiabatic column saturated virtually all the way\nup. The best chance for an afternoon or evening thunderstorm \ntoday is southwest, closest to the warm front, and the Storm \nPrediction area has a Marginal Risk and a Mesoscale Discussion \ninto this evening to the southwest, close to but not in our \nnortheast Kentucky counties.\n\nWith rainfall amounts of generally 1.5 to 2 inches forecast across \nthe middle Ohio Valley through tonight, and locally heavier \ndownpours also possible, a Flood Watch for Flash FLooding \nremains in effect there through tonight until 8 AM Saturday. By \nthen, the initial series of mid-level flat waves should be \nthrough, but the surface low will still be west of the area, \nwith its warm front extending southeastward along the Tug Fork.\n\nAnother round of mid-level flat waves amid ongoing south to \nsouthwest flow will push the surface low into the Great lakes and \nthe warm front up into the middle Ohio Valley by Sunday morning. \nWith a psuedo cold front approaching from the west, showers are \nlikely once again Saturday and Saturday night after a brief lull \nearly. Further, daytime heating and the surface boundaries will \ncontribute to convective initiation Saturday afternoon and early \nSaturday evening, although the area is not currently outlooked\nfor any severe weather threat.\n\nRainfall Saturday and Saturday night, including embedded heavier \nthunderstorms or just downpours, could continue or even expand the \npotential for flooding further in time, and even farther east. Yet \nanother wave is forecast to cross late Sunday and Sunday night,\nwith a weaker surface flow and a more diffuse array of surface \nboundaries. This is likely to bring yet another round of \nshowers and late day thunderstorms, the timing of the waves \nperhaps a little behind the peak daytime heating cycle, \ncompared with the earlier waves.\n\nThe Sunday/Sunday night flat waves push the east-coast ridge \noffshore and turn the mid/upper-level flow to the west to southwest, \nbut with the confluence zone between the northern and southern \nstreams right over the forecast area. With even another series \nof flat waves coming northeastward into the confluence zone \nMonday, and then turning eastward through it Monday night, and \nlingering surface boundaries, the potential for showers and \nmainly diurnally-driven thunderstorms with better diurnal timing\nof the flat waves again, continues. All the rainfall will need \nmonitored for possible excessive runoff throughout the Holiday \nweekend.\n\nThe Weather Prediction center keeps the Marginal Risk for Excessive \nRainfall out the forecast area Saturday, but envelops the forecast \narea with it on Sunday, and then again Monday except to limit the \nnorthern extent along the confluence zone along north-central \nportions of the forecast area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFor the mid and latter portion of next week, an east-coast long-wave \ntrough amplifies as long-wave ridging builds at all levels \nnorthwest of the forecast area. This acts to drive the \nconfluence zone and surface boundary, and hence the chance for \nmainly diurnal showers and thunderstorms by then, south of the \nforecast area. As such, next weekend is beginning to look like \nthe better pick for outdoor activities.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAfter a cool afternoon due to the extensive cloud cover and \nrainfall, lows and highs will be near normal Saturday, and then\na bit above normal through the balance of the Holiday weekend, \nand much of the upcoming work week. Lows do trend back toward \nnormal late next week amid a drier regime, as the building ridge\nand downstream east coast trough have trended a bit farther west\nin the latest models.",
